1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,580
In 1994,

2
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:03,771
a thirty-year-old Wall Street vice president

3
00:00:03,771 --> 00:00:05,874
had to decide whether to give

4
00:00:05,874 --> 00:00:08,049
up a massive mid-year bonus to

5
00:00:08,049 --> 00:00:10,080
sell books on an obscure new

6
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,110
network called the internet.

7
00:00:12,110 --> 00:00:14,362
His boss suggested he think it

8
00:00:14,362 --> 00:00:16,388
over for forty-eight hours.

9
00:00:16,388 --> 00:00:18,865
So Jeff Bezos pictured himself at

10
00:00:18,865 --> 00:00:21,117
age eighty looking back on his

11
00:00:21,117 --> 00:00:21,492
life,

12
00:00:21,492 --> 00:00:24,194
aiming to minimize the total regrets

13
00:00:24,194 --> 00:00:25,320
he would carry.

14
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:26,107
At eighty,

15
00:00:26,107 --> 00:00:28,389
he knew he would never regret

16
00:00:28,389 --> 00:00:30,749
missing one Wall Street bonus,

17
00:00:30,749 --> 00:00:32,953
and he would not even regret

18
00:00:32,953 --> 00:00:34,448
trying and failing.

19
00:00:34,448 --> 00:00:36,808
The one thing that would haunt

20
00:00:36,808 --> 00:00:39,090
him forever was never trying.

21
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:42,521
Cornell psychologists Thomas Gilovich and Victoria

22
00:00:42,521 --> 00:00:44,511
Medvec found in 1994 that our

23
00:00:44,511 --> 00:00:46,570
actions hurt more in the short

24
00:00:46,570 --> 00:00:46,913
term,

25
00:00:46,913 --> 00:00:48,285
but over a lifetime,

26
00:00:48,285 --> 00:00:50,893
people's biggest regrets are about the

27
00:00:50,893 --> 00:00:52,540
things they never tried.

28
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:55,309
Whenever short-term comfort tries to talk

29
00:00:55,309 --> 00:00:57,268
you out of a calculated leap,

30
00:00:57,268 --> 00:00:59,834
let your eighty-year-old self cast the

31
00:00:59,834 --> 00:01:00,780
deciding vote.
